      "id": "equation-sheet",
      "title": "Physics equation sheet",
      "summary": "All 35 equations on AQA's Physics equation sheet, grouped by topic, with what every symbol stands for and its unit. The six marked HT are for Higher tier papers only.",
      "status": "given",
      "statusNote": "Follows AQA's sheet for June 2027, which prints all 35. Ofqual's decision of 5 May 2026 keeps the sheet, unchanged, for exams from 2028, as the equations students must be able to use and apply but are \"not required to memorise\"; AQA publishes each year's by 1 September of the year before. No question will ask you just to copy one down: the marks are for choosing the right equation, rearranging it and using the units.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "name": "AQA GCSE Physics equation sheet (for 2027)",
          "url": "https://www.aqa.org.uk/filestore/physics/AQA-GCSE-Science-8463-INS-AI-V2.3.pdf"
        },
        {
          "name": "AQA GCSE Physics specification (8463)",
          "url": "https://filestore.aqa.org.uk/resources/physics/specifications/AQA-8463-SP-2016.PDF",
          "where": "Section 9, Appendix A"
        },
        {
          "name": "Ofqual: formulae and equation sheets from 2028",
          "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/proposed-changes-to-the-assessment-of-mathematics-physics-and-combined-science-gcses/outcome/decisions-proposed-changes-to-the-assessment-of-mathematics-physics-and-combined-science-gcses"
        }
      ],
      "furtherReading": [
        {
          "name": "BBC Bitesize: Physics (AQA)",
          "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/examspecs/zsc9rdm"
        }
